Falsifies `system_tests::user::receive_capability_attack_tests::a_death_message_received_by_receive_capability_delivers_no_capability_whichever_side_parks_first`.

Architecture: all three (the change is in shared scheduler code, `kernel/src/sched.rs`).

The defect this restores is the receiver-first order of a death message: `deliver_death` drops
`[event, tid, pc, addr, 0]` into a parked supervisor's mailbox, and with the `cap_delivered` default
reverted `ipc_receive_capability` returns it unchanged, so `x1` is the dead thread's id, where the corpse-first
order returns `NO_CAP`.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(SUPERVISOR_MSG[1], abi::rendezvous::NO_CAP, "a death message handed the
supervisor x1 = <tid> on the receiver-first order ...")`. The event check before it stays green, which
makes the red about `x1` and not a fixture that never delivered; the `x2 == child` check after it
would also fail under the patch (the raw mailbox puts the pc in `x2`), and is not the headline.

The patch is the hunk of milestone 634 (a plain SEND received by RECEIVE_CAP never hands the receiver a
sender-chosen slot), byte for byte: `ipc_receive_capability`'s receiver-first read is the one place all three deliveries
(a plain SEND, an interrupt signal, a death message) meet, so reverting it is the only defect that
puts back what this test pins. That the same revert turns the death test red is the evidence that this
test, and not only 634's, reaches the default. The hunk's context cites milestone 151 (notification
objects: async multiplexing without wait-any), whose bound-notification tag the fix exempts, and milestone 706 (a `CALL` server can tell a Reply from a delegation),
whose `x4` comment sits in the hunk's context.

Replayed on 2026-10-03 (UTC) by the lane for milestone 714 (the sibling RECEIVE_CAP paths get a receiver-first test); see the roadmap block for the exit code and
the assertion line.

Regenerated 2026-10-11 (UTC) for milestone 840 (the scheduler file is split along its seams):
the same lines, moved out of `kernel/src/sched.rs` into `kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs` unchanged, so the defect is
the same and only the path and line numbers differ.

diff --git a/kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs b/kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs
index c59bccd96..dfd1371fa 100644
--- a/kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs
@@ -677,17 +677,7 @@
             // receive, `abi::rendezvous::REPLY_DELIVERED` when a CALL's Reply was installed
             // (milestone 706, written by `ipc_call_badged`), and `0` for every other delivery a
             // sender or the kernel's death path makes (milestone 151).
-            // **x1 is NO_CAPABILITY unless a capability was installed for this delivery** (milestone 634,
-            // fatal risk 7). A plain SEND that reached us parked drops its three words straight into
-            // the mailbox, so m[1] is the sender's chosen word; returning it would hand a sender a
-            // slot number where a CALL server reads a reply slot. A bound-notification delivery is
-            // exempt: its x4 == BOUND and its m[1] is the notification word, not a sender's choice.
-            let m = t.mailbox;
-            if t.capability_delivered || m[4] == abi::notification::BOUND {
-                m
-            } else {
-                [m[0], NO_CAPABILITY, m[1], m[3], 0]
-            }
+            t.mailbox
         }
     }
 }
